# Linux System Administration – Practical Assignment

## Project Overview
This project documents my hands-on practice with Linux system diagnostics and monitoring using Kali Linux running in Oracle VirtualBox.

## Objectives
To understand how Linux systems provide information about hardware, memory, storage, processes, network connections and system logs.

## Commands Practised

1. `uname -a` – Displays kernel and system information.
2. `uptime` – Shows how long the system has been running.
3. `lscpu` – Displays CPU information.
4. `free -h` – Shows memory usage.
5. `df -h` – Displays disk space usage.
6. `lsblk` – Lists storage devices and partitions.
7. `ps aux` – Displays running processes.
8. `top` – Monitors processes and system resources.
9. `vmstat` – Reports memory, CPU and system activity.
10. `iostat` – Displays CPU and disk I/O statistics.
11. `dmesg` – Displays kernel messages.
12. `netstat -tuln` – Displays listening network ports.
13. `ss -tuln` – Shows socket and listening-port information.
14. `journalctl` – Displays system journal logs.
15. `sar` – Reports system activity statistics.

## Skills Practised
- Linux command-line navigation
- System monitoring
- CPU and memory diagnostics
- Storage monitoring
- Process management
- Network diagnostics
- System log inspection

## Learning Outcome
This practical improved my understanding of Linux system administration and introduced me to useful diagnostic tools relevant to defensive cybersecurity.

